<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The Android 12+ platform splash icon, deliberately invisible.

  WHY AN EMPTY DRAWABLE RATHER THAN NO DRAWABLE AT ALL.

  From API 31 the window manager stops honouring windowBackground for the
  launch and draws its own splash screen: a ground colour with an icon centred
  on it. That icon is not optional. Leaving windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon
  unset does not mean "no icon" — it means "use android:icon", which is
  @mipmap/ic_launcher, the SQUARE launcher mark.

  That fallback is the two-logo bug. The square icon appeared for the length of
  the platform splash, then React mounted and SplashScreen drew the wide
  493x124 wordmark: two different marks back to back, at the first thing anyone
  sees. ../values/styles.xml explains at length that the platform splash is
  given no icon, and that instruction had no way to take effect while the
  attribute was simply absent.

  The wordmark cannot be handed to the attribute instead. Android 12+ masks the
  icon into a circle at a fixed size, so a 4:1 wordmark would be cropped to the
  few letters in its middle — worse than the square mark, not better.

  So the icon is drawn and is fully transparent. The customer sees the white
  ground alone until React draws the wordmark on it, which is one mark, once,
  and is exactly what the pre-12 launch drawable (./zigly_splash.xml) puts on
  screen. The two paths now agree.

  @android:color/transparent rather than a 0x00000000 shape: it is the
  platform's own transparent, needs no size, and cannot be mistaken for
  artwork that failed to load.
-->
<color xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:color="@android:color/transparent" />
